{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"this-binding","title":"The this Keyword","group":"Functions & Scope","order":17,"html":"<p class=\"lead\"><code>this</code> is decided by <strong>how a function is called</strong>, not where it is written — except in arrow functions, which borrow it from their surroundings.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-numbers\"></i>The four binding rules (+ arrows)</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Call style</th><th><code>this</code> is</th><th>Priority</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>new Fn()</code></td><td>The newly created object</td><td>1 (highest)</td></tr><tr><td><code>fn.call(obj)</code>, <code>fn.apply(obj)</code>, <code>fn.bind(obj)()</code></td><td><code>obj</code> (explicit)</td><td>2</td></tr><tr><td><code>obj.method()</code></td><td><code>obj</code> (implicit — the object left of the dot)</td><td>3</td></tr><tr><td><code>fn()</code> plain call</td><td><code>undefined</code> in strict mode, <code>globalThis</code> in sloppy mode</td><td>4 (default)</td></tr><tr><td>Arrow function</td><td><code>this</code> of the enclosing scope — never rebound</td><td>ignores all rules</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-unlink\"></i>Implicit binding and how it is lost</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const user = {\n  name: \"Asha\",\n  hello() { return `Hi, I'm ${this?.name}`; },\n};\nconsole.log(user.hello());          // this = user\n\nconst detached = user.hello;        // just a function now\nconsole.log(detached());            // plain call → this is undefined (strict)\n\nconst other = { name: \"Ravi\", hello: user.hello };\nconsole.log(other.hello());         // this = other</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Hi, I'm Asha\nHi, I'm undefined\nHi, I'm Ravi</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Strict vs sloppy</div><p>These examples run as an ES module, which is <strong>always strict</strong>: a plain call gets <code>this === undefined</code>. In an old-style sloppy script the same call would get the global object (<code>window</code> in browsers) — the source of many silent bugs.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function strictThis() { return this; }\nconst sloppyThis = new Function(\"return this\");   // Function-constructor code is sloppy\nconsole.log(strictThis());\nconsole.log(sloppyThis() === globalThis);\nconsole.log(this);                                  // top level of a module</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>undefined\ntrue\nundefined</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-tool\"></i>Lost <code>this</code> in callbacks — and the fixes</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Timer {\n  constructor() { this.ticks = 0; }\n  startBroken() {\n    return [1, 2].map(function () { return this?.ticks; });\n  }\n  startArrow() {\n    return [1, 2].map(() =&gt; ++this.ticks);   // arrow: this = the Timer\n  }\n  startBound() {\n    return [1, 2].map(function () { return this.ticks; }.bind(this));\n  }\n}\nconst t = new Timer();\nconsole.log(t.startBroken(), t.startArrow(), t.startBound());</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ undefined, undefined ] [ 1, 2 ] [ 2, 2 ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-alert-triangle\"></i>Arrow functions as methods: a trap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const counter = {\n  count: 10,\n  arrow: () =&gt; typeof this,          // this of the module scope → undefined\n  regular() { return this.count; },\n};\nconsole.log(counter.arrow(), counter.regular());</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>undefined 10</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-plus\"></i><code>new</code> binding</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>function Person(name) {\n  this.name = name;      // `this` is the new object\n}\nconst p = new Person(\"Mei\");\nconsole.log(p, p instanceof Person);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Person { name: 'Mei' } true</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How is the value of this determined?</summary><div><p>By the call site, in priority order: <code>new</code> → the new object; <code>call</code>/<code>apply</code>/<code>bind</code> → the given object; method call <code>obj.f()</code> → <code>obj</code>; plain call → <code>undefined</code> (strict) or the global object (sloppy). Arrow functions have no own <code>this</code> and use the enclosing scope's.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why does passing obj.method as a callback lose this?</summary><div><p>Passing <code>obj.method</code> passes only the function value; the callback is later invoked as a plain call with no object before the dot, so <code>this</code> becomes <code>undefined</code>. Fix with an arrow wrapper <code>() =&gt; obj.method()</code>, <code>obj.method.bind(obj)</code>, or class fields with arrow functions.</p></div></details></section>"}